source.explanation:
. Compiler Error
Explanation: The error arises because non-static member functions cannot be called without an object. Additionally, you
cannot use the static keyword in the function definition outside the class unless the function is also declared static inside
the class declaration.
Correct version:
class Test {
public:
    static void fun();
};
void Test::fun() {
    std::cout << "fun() is static\n";


}
This fixes the issue because now fun() is explicitly declared as static.
Hence, the original program will not compile, leading to a compiler error.

